## KMP_BLOCKTIME=infinite — Detailed Verification

matmul_2 (6 paired comparisons):
  Pair 1: KMP_inf=543757 vs default=597683 → 9.0% better
  Pair 2: KMP_inf=543961 vs default=603147 → 9.8% better
  Pair 3: KMP_inf=543259 vs default=604176 → 10.1% better
  Pair 4: KMP_inf=542656 vs default=603926 → 10.1% better
  Pair 5: KMP_inf=547727 vs default=602659 → 9.1% better
  Pair 6: KMP_inf=540562 vs default=599586 → 9.8% better
  Average: 543654 vs 601863 → 9.7% improvement

matmul_3 (6 paired comparisons):
  Pair 1: KMP_inf=547340 vs default=601831 → 9.1% better
  Pair 2: KMP_inf=548326 vs default=600419 → 8.7% better
  Pair 3: KMP_inf=548677 vs default=596039 → 7.9% better
  Pair 4: KMP_inf=545380 vs default=600025 → 9.1% better
  Pair 5: KMP_inf=545837 vs default=600787 → 9.1% better
  Pair 6: KMP_inf=547350 vs default=603381 → 9.3% better
  Average: 547152 vs 600414 → 8.9% improvement

## Mechanism
KMP_BLOCKTIME controls how long OpenMP worker threads spin-wait before sleeping.
Default (KMP_BLOCKTIME=0) puts threads to sleep immediately after parallel region.
KMP_BLOCKTIME=infinite keeps threads in busy-wait, giving near-zero synchronization
latency for the next parallel region. This especially helps m2/m3 which have more
parallel regions per computation (smaller per-thread work = more sync overhead).

