--- SESSION 69 ANALYSIS ---

*** NEW BEST: post-RA-scheduler=1 ***
LLVM's post-register-allocation scheduling pass is OFF by default.
Enabling it with --post-RA-scheduler=1 improves m2 and m3 by ~3%.
The second scheduling pass reorders instructions after register assignment,
improving FMA pipeline utilization in the inner micro-kernel.

m2 postRA+fold-alias median (6 runs): ~1.39x (was 1.42x, ~3% improvement)
m3 postRA median (5 runs): ~1.10x (was 1.13x, ~3% improvement)

fold-memref-alias-ops adds small additional improvement for m2 (~1% on top of postRA).
For m3, fold-alias is NEUTRAL-to-slightly-worse (more complex address math counteracts benefit).

misched=ilpmax: HURTS m2/m3 badly (wrong scheduling for promoted memcpy patterns).
Only postRA alone benefits m2/m3. Combined misched+postRA: MUCH WORSE.

Micro-kernel findings:
  8x4x8: WORSE across all matmuls. More M unrolling doesn't help.
  4x4x16: CATASTROPHIC. Too few outputs (16 vs 32) per micro-kernel = 2x loop overhead.
  2x8x16: CATASTROPHIC. Same problem as 4x4x16.
  Conclusion: 4x8x8 is the optimal micro-kernel for AVX2 f64 outerproduct.

Other findings:
  NC=4: MUCH WORSE — too many NC loop iterations offset L1 benefit
  m1 KC=48+promA: WORSE — promotion copy overhead > L1 cache benefit for m1
  newgvn: NEUTRAL for all matmuls
  fold-alias + arith-unsigned: NEUTRAL alone, small benefit combined with postRA for m2

NEW BEST CONFIGURATIONS:
  m1: ~1.17x (claude1500 + postRA + opt + KMP_BLOCKTIME=infinite)
    Submit: KMP_BLOCKTIME=infinite LLVM_OPTS="--post-RA-scheduler=1" sbatch --export=ALL run.sh -opt -1 claude1500
